  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure builds in from the west through \nmost of this week with sunny skies. Building hts as upper ridge\nstrengthens sends temps soaring through the 80s and nearing 90\nfor some interior locales (record high climate section below). \nA dry back door front moves into nrn parts of ENC later today \nwith some increasing cloud cover and slightly cooler temps \nbehind it, but quickly retreats north by early Friday with \nanother surge of swrly flow ahead of much stronger front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Trend of a delayed frontal progression\ncontinues, with majority of guidance suggesting a low pressure\nforms offshore as the front encounters the higher baroclinicity\nof the Gulf Stream. In addition, stratiform rain chances \ncontinue increasing behind the front Saturday. There seem to be \nthree camps of model guidance, ALL of which result in hazardous \nmarine conditions and some level of coastal concerns as high \nbuilding in pinches the pressure gradient over ENC. \n\n1. The first and most likely outcome (would currently give this\na 60% chance of occurring) is a low pressure system forms \npretty soon after the cold front reaches the Gulf Stream, as \nearly as 00Z Sunday. The is supported by the 00Z Thursday runs \nof Euro (deterministic and ensemble), Euro AI, RRFS, and NAM. \nThere is still a wide range of outcomes for strength, but weak \nsteering flow should allow the low pressure system to meander \noffshore, tapping into the warm gulf stream waters and \ndeepening. Eventual track and strength are still very uncertain.\nWhile unlikely, an eventual westward track back towards ENC is \nnot out of the question. \n\n2. The second outcome (would currently give this a 30% chance \nof occuring) is the front stalls along the Gulf Stream, forming \na low hundreds of miles offshore a later on (around Mon-Tue). \nThis outcome is supported by the deterministic GFS, some GEFS \nmembers and CMC. this outcome would still bring strong NE winds,\nbut shorter in duration and less wave energy reaching coastal \nareas.\n\n3. The third and least likely outcome (would currently give \nthis a 10% chance of occurring) is the front stalls offshore and\nnever generates a low pressure system. Strong NE winds would \nstill occur as the high builds in, but like scenario 2, winds \nwould be shorter duration and wave energy would be on the lower \nend. \n\nRegardless of which scenario plays out, there is HIGH confidence\nof marine impacts, and potential for waves 7+ ft reaching\nportions of the OBX. Winds advisory potential exists for the \nOBX zones, as well as perhaps portions of the adjacent coastal \nmainland. Will have to watch for minor coastal flood concerns \nfor srn portions of Pamlico Sound and the coastal areas of the \nOBX with the sustained NE flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Coolest and driest airmass for the fall season \nthus far builds in behind the front Sat night and beyond with \ntemps taking a nosedive with highs in the 60s and lows in the \n40s (inland) and 50s (beaches). However, depending how offshore \nthe front makes it, could keep things a bit milder than fcst if \nsome clouds advect in from offshore keeping the nighttime mins \na bit warmer than advertised.",
